      "content": "Yes, historians widely agree that the veto power is a fundamental and valid structural flaw that severely limits the UN's ability to act decisively without broad global consensus. This design choice inherently prioritizes the survival of the original major powers over the immediate needs of the international community, making the organization often reactive rather than proactive. Whether this is viewed as a \"broken\" system or an \"inevitable\" compromise depends largely on whether one prioritizes the ideal of global justice or the pragmatic reality of great power politics.",
